  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 153 AM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nA tightening pressure gradient will remain across much of Iowa on \nSaturday as the surface cyclone over Canada deepens. As a result. \nSaturday will be breezier than Friday. In addition, this is expected \nto be yet another warm day, with highs once again in the mid to \nupper 70s as stronger mixing mixes down warmer temperatures. At \nleast one more day of these well-above normal (near +15 degrees!) is \nin store on Sunday as the ridge axis translates eastward. \n\nBy Monday, a cool front associated with a surface cyclone over \nCanada will sag through the CWA. Model guidance remains split on \ntiming for this front, resulting in a large spread for highs, \nespecially in the northwestern half of the CWA. While it may be \npossible for highs as warm as 65 degrees (45-85% north to south), a \ncooldown is likely for at least part of the area. In addition, \nMonday brings our next chance of measurable rainfall, though chances \nare low (10-30%). The majority of these chances will be west of the \nMississippi, while areas east of the Mississippi will have below a \n50% chance for measurable rainfall. For areas that do see measurable \nrain, accumulations up to a tenth of an inch are possible (20-40%). \n\nTuesday and beyond will bring additional chances for rain and cooler \ntemperatures.",
